// Syllabus: shared/curriculum.js, weekCatalog and moduleGuides 9–12.
// The original weeks/, guides/, and resources/ sources are absent from this checkout.
export const chapters09to12 = {
  9: { sections: [
    { id: 'w9-tree-model-and-dfs', title: 'A tree is a collection of smaller trees', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Think of a folder containing two smaller folders. You can understand the whole hierarchy by understanding the current folder and applying the same question to each child. A binary tree makes that idea precise: a node contains a value and at most two child references, conventionally called left and right. A missing child is null. A leaf has no children. The root has no parent, and each other node has exactly one parent. These conditions matter: if two parents share a child, or a child points back to an ancestor, you have a graph rather than an ordinary tree. The traversal code in this section assumes a finite tree with no shared nodes or cycles.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Before choosing an algorithm, separate the shape from the stored values. The root could contain 8 and its left child 20; that is a perfectly valid binary tree. There is no search ordering rule yet. Height describes the longest downward path, while width describes how many nodes appear across a level. We will measure depth in nodes: an empty tree has depth zero and a leaf has depth one. Some texts count edges instead, giving a leaf height zero. Neither convention is wrong, but mixing them makes every answer off by one. State the convention before tracing or writing a base case.' },
      { type: 'diagram', text: '        A\n       / \\\n      B   C\n     / \\\n    D   E\nPreorder:  A B D E C   (node, left, right)\nInorder:   D B E A C   (left, node, right)\nPostorder: D E B C A   (left, right, node)\nDepth in nodes: 3. Maximum level width: 2.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Depth-first search follows one branch before moving to its sibling. The three traversal names tell you when to do the work at a node. Preorder handles the parent before its descendants, which is useful for copying or serializing a hierarchy. Inorder places the parent between its two subtrees; it becomes sorted only when the tree also satisfies a binary search tree ordering rule. Postorder handles descendants first, making it natural when a parent needs answers from both children. The recursive call stack remembers unfinished parents. Returning from the left subtree means “resume this parent and continue with its right subtree,” not “start again at the root.”' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function orders(root) {\n  const pre = [], ino = [], post = [];\n  function visit(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    pre.push(node.value);\n    visit(node.left);\n    ino.push(node.value);\n    visit(node.right);\n    post.push(node.value);\n  }\n  visit(root);\n  return { pre, ino, post };\n}\nconst leaf = value => ({ value, left: null, right: null });\nconst tree = { value: "A", left: { value: "B", left: leaf("D"), right: leaf("E") }, right: leaf("C") };\nconsole.log(orders(tree)); //=> { pre: ["A","B","D","E","C"], ino: ["D","B","E","A","C"], post: ["D","E","B","C","A"] }\nconsole.log(orders(null)); //=> { pre: [], ino: [], post: [] }' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Trace the moment visit(B) returns: D and E have already finished, so B can enter the postorder result. A is still suspended with its right call unstarted. That distinction is the key to reading recursion without getting lost. Every non-null node is entered exactly once, and each entry performs constant work apart from its child calls. Counting those entries proves Θ(n) traversal time. The recursion stack holds one root-to-current path, so auxiliary stack space is O(h). Storing three result arrays costs Θ(n) output space as well. A balanced shape has h proportional to log n, but a chain has h equal to n; JavaScript recursion can overflow on such a chain.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'For root 4 with left child 9 and right child 2, where 9 has only right child 7, write all three DFS orders. Is the inorder output sorted? Explain what this reveals about binary trees.', hints: ['Draw explicit null children so that the left and right positions stay distinct.', 'Move the action before, between, or after the recursive child calls.'], solution: 'Preorder is [4,9,7,2], inorder is [9,7,4,2], and postorder is [7,9,2,4]. Inorder is not sorted: a binary tree constrains the number of children, not the ordering of values. Sorted inorder requires the additional BST invariant.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w9-subtree-answers', title: 'Let each subtree return exactly the answer its parent needs', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Maximum depth is a good first example of designing a recursive contract. Ask a child for its maximum depth, not for its entire traversal. If the left answer is L and the right answer is R, the current tree has depth 1 + max(L,R). The empty tree returns zero, which makes a leaf return one without a special leaf case. This is postorder reasoning even though the implementation may not contain a named postorder traversal. The parent combines completed child answers. When a recursive solution feels mysterious, write the sentence “this call returns … for the subtree rooted here” and check that every return satisfies it.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'On the A–B–D/E example, D and E each return one. B combines them into two. C returns one. Finally A computes 1 + max(2,1), giving three. The recurrence for runtime is T(node) = T(left) + T(right) + O(1). Those subtree sizes partition the remaining nodes, so the total is Θ(n), not O(2^n). Two recursive calls do not imply exponential time when they operate on disjoint pieces. This is a useful complexity habit beyond trees: count how often the same state is solved, not just how many call expressions appear in the source.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function maximumDepth(root) {\n  if (root === null) return 0;\n  return 1 + Math.max(maximumDepth(root.left), maximumDepth(root.right));\n}\nfunction maximumDepthIterative(root) {\n  if (root === null) return 0;\n  const stack = [[root, 1]];\n  let best = 0;\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    const [node, depth] = stack.pop();\n    best = Math.max(best, depth);\n    if (node.right !== null) stack.push([node.right, depth + 1]);\n    if (node.left !== null) stack.push([node.left, depth + 1]);\n  }\n  return best;\n}\nfunction invert(root) {\n  if (root === null) return null;\n  const oldLeft = root.left;\n  root.left = invert(root.right);\n  root.right = invert(oldLeft);\n  return root;\n}\nconst tree = { value: 1, left: { value: 2, left: null, right: null }, right: null };\nconsole.log(maximumDepth(tree), maximumDepthIterative(tree)); //=> 2 2\nconsole.log(invert(tree).right.value); //=> 2\nconsole.log(maximumDepth(null), invert(null)); //=> 0 null' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The iterative version stores the information recursion would have carried: a node and its depth. Push right before left if you want left processed first, because a stack removes the most recently pushed item. In a binary tree this pending stack is O(h): each active ancestor contributes at most one deferred sibling. Inversion asks a different question. It exchanges the two child subtrees at every node, changing the original structure. Save the old left reference before overwriting it, or a later assignment can accidentally reuse the new child. Values do not change, and an inorder traversal of the inverted result generally changes order. If callers need the original tree preserved, construct new nodes instead and count Θ(n) allocation space.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A useful correctness proof is structural induction. The empty subtree has the claimed answer by the base case. Assume both smaller child subtrees return correct depths; every root-to-leaf path then consists of this root plus a path through exactly one child. Taking the larger child depth and adding one therefore returns the correct answer. Inversion has a similar proof: if both children are correctly mirrored, exchanging their positions mirrors the whole tree. This is more convincing than saying “recursion works,” because it identifies the exact property transferred from children to parent.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'A learner writes return maximumDepth(node.left) + maximumDepth(node.right) + 1. Give a counterexample and repair the recurrence. Then explain whether their formula computes anything useful.', hints: ['Try a root with two leaf children.', 'A path chooses one child, but a node count includes both subtrees.'], solution: 'For a root with two leaves the formula returns 3, while the maximum depth is 2. Depth needs 1 + Math.max(leftDepth,rightDepth). The sum formula is instead a correct node-count recurrence when its empty case returns zero, because it counts every node in both disjoint subtrees plus the root.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w9-level-order', title: 'BFS makes levels visible', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Use breadth-first search when the question talks about levels, nearest descendants, or the first layer satisfying a condition. A queue promises first-in, first-out behavior. Start with the root, remove the oldest pending node, then append its children. A child cannot jump ahead of a previously discovered node from the current level. To return a separate array for every level, freeze the current frontier before expanding it. If you keep checking a growing queue length inside the level loop, newly appended children will be consumed as if they belonged to their parents’ level.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function levelOrder(root) {\n  if (root === null) return [];\n  const levels = [];\n  let frontier = [root];\n  while (frontier.length > 0) {\n    const values = [];\n    const next = [];\n    for (const node of frontier) {\n      values.push(node.value);\n      if (node.left !== null) next.push(node.left);\n      if (node.right !== null) next.push(node.right);\n    }\n    levels.push(values);\n    frontier = next;\n  }\n  return levels;\n}\nconst tree = { value: 1, left: { value: 2, left: null, right: null }, right: { value: 3, left: { value: 4, left: null, right: null }, right: null } };\nconsole.log(levelOrder(tree)); //=> [[1],[2,3],[4]]\nconsole.log(levelOrder(null)); //=> []' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Initially frontier contains [1]. Processing it produces values [1] and next [2,3]. The next iteration processes exactly those two nodes, producing values [2,3] and next [4]. After [4], next is empty and traversal stops. At every iteration the invariant is that frontier contains precisely one level in left-to-right order. Induction on levels proves that every child enters the following frontier exactly once. This implementation uses Θ(n) time, O(w) working memory across the current and next frontiers, and Θ(n) output space. It avoids repeatedly shifting an array, whose front removal can move all remaining entries.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A head-index queue is also a good time-efficient implementation, but be accurate about memory. Incrementing head does not remove old array slots; if you keep the same array for the whole traversal, it retains n node references. Its active queue can be O(w) while its actual backing storage is O(n). Separate frontiers make the width bound explicit. Compare two extremes: a long chain has width one and depth n, while a nearly complete tree has depth O(log n) and a final level containing Θ(n) nodes. Neither DFS nor BFS is always more memory efficient. Choose based on both the shape and the answer you need, and separate returned arrays from temporary storage in your explanation.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'You need maximum depth for a chain of 100,000 nodes and then a level-order result for a broad tree. Which implementations above would you choose, and what memory would you report?', hints: ['JavaScript does not guarantee a call stack large enough for the chain.', 'Output memory and working memory answer different questions.'], solution: 'Use maximumDepthIterative for the chain to avoid recursive call-stack overflow; the pending stack is constant-sized for a chain, though its general bound is O(h). Use frontier BFS for the level-order result. It requires O(w) working memory and Θ(n) returned values. A broad tree can have w = Θ(n), so BFS is not inherently constant-space.' }
    ] }
  ] },
  10: { sections: [
    { id: 'w10-bst-order', title: 'A BST turns comparisons into safe decisions', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A binary search tree adds a global ordering promise to the binary tree shape. Under the strict rule used here, every value anywhere in a node’s left subtree is smaller than the node’s value, and every value anywhere in its right subtree is larger. The word anywhere matters. Checking only immediate children misses violations deeper down. We will store finite numbers, reject duplicate insertions, and represent absent children with null. Other duplicate policies are possible, such as storing a count beside each distinct key, but search, insertion, validation, and deletion must all agree on the same policy.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Imagine searching for 6 in a tree rooted at 8. Since 6 is smaller, the entire right subtree can be discarded: all of its values exceed 8. At node 3, move right, because its left subtree contains only values below 3. At node 6, stop. This is the same elimination reasoning that made binary search useful, but a tree’s shape controls how many comparisons remain. Search takes O(h), where h is height. A balanced tree gives O(log n), while inserting 1,2,3,4 in order into an ordinary BST creates a rightward chain and makes the worst case Θ(n). Merely naming a structure “BST” does not establish balance.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function insertBST(root, value) {\n  const node = { value, left: null, right: null };\n  if (root === null) return node;\n  let current = root;\n  while (true) {\n    if (value === current.value) return root;\n    const side = value < current.value ? "left" : "right";\n    if (current[side] === null) { current[side] = node; return root; }\n    current = current[side];\n  }\n}\nfunction searchBST(root, target) {\n  let node = root;\n  while (node !== null) {\n    if (node.value === target) return true;\n    node = target < node.value ? node.left : node.right;\n  }\n  return false;\n}\nfunction validBST(root, low = -Infinity, high = Infinity) {\n  if (root === null) return true;\n  if (!(low < root.value && root.value < high)) return false;\n  return validBST(root.left, low, root.value) && validBST(root.right, root.value, high);\n}\nlet root = null;\nfor (const value of [8,3,10,1,6,14,4,7]) root = insertBST(root, value);\nconsole.log(searchBST(root, 6), searchBST(root, 5)); //=> true false\nconsole.log(validBST(root)); //=> true\nconst invalid = { value: 8, left: { value: 3, left: null, right: { value: 9, left: null, right: null } }, right: null };\nconsole.log(validBST(invalid)); //=> false' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Insertion follows the same comparisons until it reaches a missing reference, then attaches a leaf. Existing ordering relationships remain unchanged, and every ancestor comparison constrains the new value to the correct interval. Validation makes those constraints explicit. At root 8 the permitted interval is (-Infinity,Infinity). Its left child inherits (-Infinity,8), and that child’s right branch inherits (3,8). A value 9 fails even though it exceeds its immediate parent 3. Bounds travel downward; boolean answers travel upward. Strict inequalities implement the duplicate policy. With finite numeric keys, Infinity is safe as a boundary sentinel; for arbitrary objects or strings, use a comparator and explicit absent bounds.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'BST inorder traversal is sorted because its left output is entirely smaller than the root and its right output entirely larger, with the same property recursively inside each subtree. That gives a second way to validate: compare each inorder value to the previous one. Do not initialize the previous value to zero, because negative keys are valid. Search and iterative insertion use O(1) auxiliary space and O(h) time. Validation visits each node at most once, taking O(n) worst-case time and O(h) call-stack space. A range query can prune subtrees outside the range; on a balanced tree its useful target is O(log n + r), where r is the number of returned values.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'A validator accepts every node whose left child is smaller and right child is larger. Construct a three-node counterexample. Explain how propagated bounds reject it and why sorted insertions still harm search performance.', hints: ['Put a descendant in the left subtree that exceeds the root.', 'Ordering correctness and shape balance are separate properties.'], solution: 'Use root 8, left child 3, and 3’s right child 9. Local checks pass, but 9 violates the root’s left-subtree bound. The recursive interval at 9 is (3,8), so bounds reject it. Sorted insertion into an unbalanced BST can still satisfy every ordering constraint while forming a height-n chain, making search linear.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w10-heap-internals', title: 'A heap keeps only the next priority at the front', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A priority queue answers a different question from a BST: which pending item should be handled next? A min-heap guarantees that a parent’s priority is no larger than either child’s. It says nothing about the relative order of siblings or unrelated branches. The array [2,7,4,9,8,6] is a valid min-heap even though it is not sorted. The shape is complete: all levels are full except possibly the last, which fills from left to right. That shape allows references to disappear entirely. For zero-based index i, children are 2i+1 and 2i+2, and the parent is floor((i-1)/2).' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Insertion appends at the next open leaf position. Only the new leaf-to-root path can violate heap order, so bubble upward while the child outranks its parent. Removing the minimum saves the root, moves the final leaf into its place, and bubbles downward. Choose the smaller child before swapping: swapping with the larger child can leave the smaller child above its new parent’s priority. Each repair follows one path of a complete tree, whose height is O(log n). Peek reads index zero in O(1); push and pop take O(log n); arbitrary membership search remains O(n). These promises are exactly what scheduling and repeated extreme selection need.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'class MinHeap {\n  constructor(compare = (a, b) => a - b) { this.items = []; this.compare = compare; }\n  get size() { return this.items.length; }\n  peek() { return this.items[0]; }\n  push(value) {\n    const a = this.items;\n    a.push(value);\n    let i = a.length - 1;\n    while (i > 0) {\n      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);\n      if (this.compare(a[i], a[p]) >= 0) break;\n      [a[i], a[p]] = [a[p], a[i]];\n      i = p;\n    }\n  }\n  pop() {\n    const a = this.items;\n    if (a.length === 0) return undefined;\n    const answer = a[0];\n    const last = a.pop();\n    if (a.length === 0) return answer;\n    a[0] = last;\n    let i = 0;\n    while (2 * i + 1 < a.length) {\n      let child = 2 * i + 1;\n      if (child + 1 < a.length && this.compare(a[child + 1], a[child]) < 0) child++;\n      if (this.compare(a[i], a[child]) <= 0) break;\n      [a[i], a[child]] = [a[child], a[i]];\n      i = child;\n    }\n    return answer;\n  }\n}\nconst heap = new MinHeap();\nfor (const value of [7,2,6,1]) heap.push(value);\nconsole.log(heap.peek(), heap.size); //=> 1 4\nconsole.log([heap.pop(), heap.pop(), heap.pop(), heap.pop(), heap.pop()]); //=> [1,2,6,7,undefined]\nconst jobs = new MinHeap((a,b) => a.priority - b.priority);\njobs.push({ name: "later", priority: 8 });\njobs.push({ name: "urgent", priority: 1 });\nconsole.log(jobs.pop().name); //=> "urgent"' },
      { type: 'diagram', text: 'Insert 1 into [2,7,6]:\nappend                 [2,7,6,1]\ncompare index 3 to 1    [2,1,6,7]\ncompare index 1 to 0    [1,2,6,7]\nPop minimum 1:\nmove last to root      [7,2,6]\nchoose smaller child 2 [2,7,6]\nThe array is a heap again, not a sorted sequence.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The repair proof is local. Before insertion all old edges satisfy the invariant; moving the smaller new key upward can only expose a violation with its next parent. Before removal, both child subtrees are already heaps. Moving the smaller child upward satisfies the current parent position, leaving only one descendant position to repair. Stop when there is no child or the parent already outranks the chosen child. Comparators must define a consistent ordering; boolean comparators are incorrect because the code needs negative, zero, and positive results. Equal priorities do not promise stable arrival order. Add an increasing sequence number as a secondary key when stable ties matter. Do not mutate an item’s priority while it remains inside the heap without a repair operation.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Building a heap by pushing n values costs O(n log n). Bottom-up heap construction can do better: sift down each internal node from the last parent to the root. Most nodes are near leaves and move very little. Summing at most n/2 nodes doing constant work, n/4 doing two levels, n/8 doing three, and so on gives O(n), since the weighted geometric series converges. This does not make extracting every item linear; n successive pops still cost O(n log n). Distinguish building the data structure from fully sorting its contents. Also avoid claiming JavaScript has a standard built-in priority queue: this module declares the structure explicitly.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Starting with min-heap [2,5,4,9,7], insert 1 and then remove the minimum. Show each swap. Why would choosing the larger child during removal be unsafe?', hints: ['The appended value starts at index 5 with parent index 2.', 'After removing the root, inspect both children before deciding where to move.'], solution: 'Insertion gives [2,5,4,9,7,1], then [2,5,1,9,7,4], then [1,5,2,9,7,4]. Removal moves 4 to the root, yielding [4,5,2,9,7], then swaps with 2 to give [2,5,4,9,7]. Swapping with the larger child 5 would leave root 5 above child 2, violating min-heap order.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w10-top-k', title: 'Top K is a boundary that moves as data arrives', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'To retain the k largest values, keep a min-heap of at most k items. This sounds backward until you identify the boundary: the smallest retained value is the easiest one to evict. A value below that boundary cannot belong among the largest k seen so far. A value above it replaces the boundary. A max-heap would put the strongest retained candidate at the root, which is the wrong candidate to discard. This “keep the worst of the winners accessible” idea appears in ranking, streaming statistics, and bounded candidate selection. The result heap is unordered; only its root has a guaranteed position.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Trace k=3 over [4,5,8,2,10]. After the first three values, the retained set is {4,5,8}, with boundary 4. Value 2 is rejected. Value 10 replaces 4, leaving {5,8,10}, with boundary 5. The third largest value is therefore 5. Duplicates count as separate occurrences here: in [9,9,2] the second largest is 9. For a stream, the answer is unavailable until k observations exist. For k=0, retaining no values is a useful top-K convention, but “zeroth largest” has no meaning. The following self-contained example returns retained values rather than pretending those two contracts are identical.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function topK(values, k) {\n  if (!Number.isInteger(k) || k < 0) throw new RangeError("k must be a nonnegative integer");\n  if (k === 0) return [];\n  const heap = [];\n  function push(value) {\n    heap.push(value);\n    let i = heap.length - 1;\n    while (i > 0) {\n      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);\n      if (heap[p] <= heap[i]) break;\n      [heap[p], heap[i]] = [heap[i], heap[p]];\n      i = p;\n    }\n  }\n  function replaceMinimum(value) {\n    heap[0] = value;\n    let i = 0;\n    while (2 * i + 1 < heap.length) {\n      let c = 2 * i + 1;\n      if (c + 1 < heap.length && heap[c + 1] < heap[c]) c++;\n      if (heap[i] <= heap[c]) break;\n      [heap[i], heap[c]] = [heap[c], heap[i]];\n      i = c;\n    }\n  }\n  for (const value of values) {\n    if (heap.length < k) push(value);\n    else if (value > heap[0]) replaceMinimum(value);\n  }\n  return heap;\n}\nconsole.log(topK([4,5,8,2,10], 3).sort((a,b) => a-b)); //=> [5,8,10]\nconsole.log(topK([9,9,2], 2)); //=> [9,9]\nconsole.log(topK([1,2], 0)); //=> []' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Prove the retained-set invariant after each input prefix. Before k elements, retain everything. Once full, a new item no greater than the minimum cannot improve the top k. If it is greater, exchanging it with the minimum produces exactly a valid largest-k multiset for the enlarged prefix. The heap merely makes this safe exchange efficient. Each update costs O(log(k+1)) in the worst case and retained storage is O(min(n,k)). The plus one avoids the misleading log 1 = 0 expression when k is one. Sorting the final answer, if requested, adds O(k log k) when at least k inputs exist. The numeric comparator is essential: JavaScript’s default sort compares string representations, so 10 can appear before 2.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'For Top K Frequent Elements, first count occurrences in a Map, then heap the distinct values using frequency as priority. If n inputs contain u distinct values, counting takes expected O(n) time and O(u) storage, and selecting takes O(u log(k+1)). Decide whether equal frequencies may appear in any order; add a tie rule if the output contract demands one. For Kth Largest Element in an Array, quickselect is another option with expected linear time, but a bounded heap is particularly natural when values arrive incrementally. BSTs fit ordered membership and range questions; heaps fit repeated next-priority questions. That recognition distinction is more useful than memorizing a single problem title.' }
    ] }
  ] },
  11: { sections: [
    { id: 'w11-graph-models', title: 'Model the relationships before traversing them', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A graph begins with a modeling decision: what is a vertex, and what relationship creates an edge? In a transport map, vertices might be stations and edges direct services. In a grid, vertices might be land cells and edges shared sides. In a social network, vertices might be people. Unlike a tree, a graph can contain cycles, disconnected regions, shared destinations, and vertices with no neighbors. Those possibilities change the algorithm. Traversing only from vertex zero does not explore an entire disconnected graph. Following neighbors without remembering previous visits can loop forever, even in a tiny triangle.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'An undirected edge means movement is allowed both ways, so an adjacency list stores both endpoints as neighbors. A directed edge stores only its stated direction. Weighted edges carry costs; an unweighted edge has an implicit equal cost, usually one. Keep these distinctions explicit before coding. An adjacency list uses O(V+E) storage and is usually convenient for sparse graphs. An adjacency matrix uses Θ(V²) storage and offers constant-time edge lookup, but enumerating one vertex’s neighbors requires scanning a row. An edge list is compact input, yet repeatedly scanning all edges to find neighbors can turn a traversal into O(VE).' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function adjacency(vertices, edges, directed = false) {\n  const graph = new Map(vertices.map(v => [v, []]));\n  for (const [a,b] of edges) {\n    if (!graph.has(a) || !graph.has(b)) throw new Error("Unknown endpoint");\n    graph.get(a).push(b);\n    if (!directed) graph.get(b).push(a);\n  }\n  return graph;\n}\nfunction components(vertices, edges) {\n  const graph = adjacency(vertices, edges);\n  const seen = new Set();\n  const groups = [];\n  for (const start of graph.keys()) {\n    if (seen.has(start)) continue;\n    const group = [], stack = [start];\n    seen.add(start);\n    while (stack.length > 0) {\n      const node = stack.pop();\n      group.push(node);\n      for (const next of graph.get(node)) {\n        if (seen.has(next)) continue;\n        seen.add(next);\n        stack.push(next);\n      }\n    }\n    groups.push(group);\n  }\n  return groups;\n}\nconsole.log(components([0,1,2,3,4], [[0,1],[1,2],[2,0],[3,3]])); //=> [[0,2,1],[3],[4]]\nconsole.log(components([], [])); //=> []\nconsole.log(adjacency(["A","B"], [["A","B"]], true).get("B")); //=> []' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The explicit vertex collection preserves isolated vertices. Building a Map only from edge endpoints would silently lose vertex 4 in this example. Array-based adjacency is excellent for dense integer labels from zero through V−1; a Map supports strings and object identity without assuming that labels are indexes. Avoid Array(n).fill([]), because every entry then refers to the same neighbor array. Create a fresh array per vertex instead. A self-loop adds the vertex as its own neighbor, and parallel edges may repeat a neighbor. Neither breaks reachability when visited marking is correct, though they still contribute to the number of adjacency entries scanned.' },
      { type: 'diagram', text: 'Vertices: 0,1,2,3,4\nEdges: 0—1, 1—2, 2—0, 3—3\nAdjacency:\n0: [1,2]   1: [0,2]   2: [1,0]   3: [3,3]   4: []\nDFS from 0 marks 0,1,2. Outer scan next starts at 3, then at 4.\nThree components; a self-loop does not add a vertex or a component.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Mark a vertex when scheduling it, not after finishing all its descendants. The invariant is that every scheduled vertex is already in seen. A triangle can therefore never schedule its starting vertex again. The outer loop starts one new traversal for each as-yet unseen vertex; that traversal reaches every vertex in its connected component and cannot cross to another component without an edge connecting them. This proves both that no component is missed and that none is counted twice. The argument is for undirected components. Applying the same outer loop to a directed graph does not compute strongly connected components, because one-way reachability is not mutual reachability.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Count work by adjacency entries. Each vertex enters the stack at most once, and its neighbor list is scanned once. An undirected edge appears twice, giving 2E scans rather than E, which is still Θ(E). Construction and full traversal take Θ(V+E) time. The graph takes Θ(V+E) storage, and seen plus stack take O(V) additional working space. Returning all groups takes O(V) output storage. Iterative DFS avoids JavaScript recursion-depth limits on a long path. The exact visit order depends on neighbor order and stack behavior; connectivity does not. A different valid traversal order should not be mistaken for an incorrect component partition.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Given vertices A,B,C,D and undirected edges A–B, B–C, C–A, explain why marking after recursive calls fails. What result should a component counter return, and why must D appear in the model?', hints: ['Follow A to B and then back to A before any call returns.', 'A vertex with no incident edge is still a vertex.'], solution: 'Delayed marking allows A to call B, B to call A, and so on before either becomes visited. Mark before exploring neighbors. The triangle forms one component and isolated D forms another, so the count is two. An adjacency structure created only from endpoints would omit D and return the wrong count.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w11-bfs-shortest-paths', title: 'BFS finds a shortest path when every step costs the same', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Reachability asks whether a route exists. Shortest unweighted path asks for the route with the fewest edges. BFS answers the latter because it explores vertices in nondecreasing distance layers: first the source, then all vertices one edge away, then two edges away. A DFS may discover a long detour before a short route; first discovery in DFS is not a shortest-path certificate. BFS first discovery is a certificate only when every edge has equal cost. A direct expensive flight and a cheap two-flight connection demonstrate why counting edges is different from minimizing weights.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Store two facts when discovering a vertex: its distance and the predecessor that discovered it. Distance tells how many edges the route uses. Predecessors let you reconstruct the actual route backward from the target, then reverse it. The source gets distance zero and a null predecessor. In the implementation below, membership in distance also serves as the visited test, so there is no separate Set to keep synchronized. Do not test distance.get(vertex) for truthiness: the source’s valid distance is zero. Map.has asks the intended question directly, and a null route distinguishes an unreachable target from the zero-edge route from a source to itself.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function shortestUnweighted(graph, source, target) {\n  if (!graph.has(source) || !graph.has(target)) return null;\n  const distance = new Map([[source, 0]]);\n  const parent = new Map([[source, null]]);\n  const queue = [source];\n  let head = 0;\n  while (head < queue.length) {\n    const node = queue[head++];\n    if (node === target) break;\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      if (distance.has(next)) continue;\n      distance.set(next, distance.get(node) + 1);\n      parent.set(next, node);\n      queue.push(next);\n    }\n  }\n  if (!distance.has(target)) return null;\n  const path = [];\n  for (let node = target; node !== null; node = parent.get(node)) path.push(node);\n  path.reverse();\n  return { distance: distance.get(target), path };\n}\nconst graph = new Map([\n  ["A", ["B","C"]], ["B", ["A","D"]], ["C", ["A","D"]],\n  ["D", ["B","C"]], ["E", []]\n]);\nconsole.log(shortestUnweighted(graph, "A", "D")); //=> { distance: 2, path: ["A","B","D"] }\nconsole.log(shortestUnweighted(graph, "A", "A")); //=> { distance: 0, path: ["A"] }\nconsole.log(shortestUnweighted(graph, "A", "E")); //=> null' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Trace the diamond A–B–D and A–C–D. Processing A discovers B and C at distance one. Processing B discovers D at distance two and records parent B. Processing C sees that D is already discovered and leaves its predecessor unchanged. There are two shortest routes, but the function returns one based on neighbor order. Marking at enqueue time prevents duplicate queue entries at the convergence point D. For correctness, suppose a vertex first discovered at distance d had a shorter route. The preceding vertex on that shorter route would lie in an earlier BFS layer and would already have discovered it, a contradiction. That is why the first distance never needs improvement here.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Worst-case runtime is O(V+E), plus O(V) for reconstruction, and auxiliary storage is O(V). The head-index queue deliberately retains processed entries, so its actual storage bound is O(V), not merely the current frontier width. In a graph this bound is natural because distances and parents already store up to V entries. Source and target labels in this example are strings; null is reserved as the predecessor terminator. If null must itself be a legal vertex label, terminate reconstruction by comparing with source instead. These small representation choices belong in the function contract, especially when adapting an interview implementation into a reusable library.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'A graph has edges S–A, A–B, B–T, S–C, C–T. A DFS explores A before C. What route might it return first, and what route length must BFS return? When would neither edge-count answer minimize travel cost?', hints: ['Follow the A branch all the way before returning to S.', 'Assign a large weight to S–C to separate edge count from route cost.'], solution: 'DFS can first return S,A,B,T, which uses three edges. BFS must return a two-edge route S,C,T. If edges have different costs, neither a first DFS route nor a minimum-edge route is guaranteed to minimize total weight; a nonnegative weighted instance calls for Dijkstra instead.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w11-grids-and-cloning', title: 'Recognize graphs even when no edge list is supplied', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Number of Islands is a component problem hidden inside a grid. Each land cell is a vertex, and orthogonally adjacent land cells share an edge. Water contributes no vertices. The important modeling detail is the neighborhood: four directions and eight directions define different graphs. We use four, so diagonally touching land is disconnected unless another path links it. You do not need to allocate a complete adjacency list; generate up to four neighbors while visiting a cell. The boundary checks and land test together decide whether an implicit edge exists. Empty grids return zero, and this version assumes a rectangular grid of string cells.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function numberOfIslands(grid) {\n  const rows = grid.length, cols = grid[0]?.length ?? 0;\n  const seen = new Set();\n  const directions = [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]];\n  let count = 0;\n  for (let r = 0; r < rows; r++) {\n    for (let c = 0; c < cols; c++) {\n      const key = r * cols + c;\n      if (grid[r][c] !== "1" || seen.has(key)) continue;\n      count++;\n      seen.add(key);\n      const stack = [[r,c]];\n      while (stack.length) {\n        const [x,y] = stack.pop();\n        for (const [dx,dy] of directions) {\n          const nx = x + dx, ny = y + dy;\n          if (nx < 0 || nx >= rows || ny < 0 || ny >= cols) continue;\n          const nextKey = nx * cols + ny;\n          if (grid[nx][ny] !== "1" || seen.has(nextKey)) continue;\n          seen.add(nextKey);\n          stack.push([nx,ny]);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return count;\n}\nconsole.log(numberOfIslands([["1","1","0"],["0","0","1"],["1","0","1"]])); //=> 3\nconsole.log(numberOfIslands([])); //=> 0' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The outer scan finds the first cell of each previously unseen island. Flood fill marks the rest of that island, so the next scan position inside it cannot increase the count. Each cell is tested by the outer scan once, and each visited land cell generates at most four neighbors. Time is O(rows × cols) and extra storage is O(rows × cols) in the worst case. A mutating implementation can mark visited land as water and remove the Set, but its stack may still hold a linear number of cells. Mutating the input does not automatically mean constant auxiliary space, and it changes the grid visible to the caller.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Clone Graph introduces a second reason to remember visits: preserving identity. Suppose A points to B twice, and B points back to A. A correct deep clone must preserve those relationships using new objects, including both references to the same cloned B. Mapping values to clones is unsafe because distinct original nodes may have equal values. Use original object references as Map keys. Allocate and record a clone before exploring its neighbors; otherwise a cycle can reenter a node whose clone has not been registered. Copying each encountered node independently also fails, because it duplicates shared destinations instead of preserving the graph structure.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function cloneGraph(start) {\n  if (start === null) return null;\n  const copies = new Map([[start, { value: start.value, neighbors: [] }]]);\n  const queue = [start];\n  for (let head = 0; head < queue.length; head++) {\n    const original = queue[head];\n    for (const neighbor of original.neighbors) {\n      if (!copies.has(neighbor)) {\n        copies.set(neighbor, { value: neighbor.value, neighbors: [] });\n        queue.push(neighbor);\n      }\n      copies.get(original).neighbors.push(copies.get(neighbor));\n    }\n  }\n  return copies.get(start);\n}\nconst a = { value: 1, neighbors: [] };\nconst b = { value: 1, neighbors: [] };\na.neighbors = [b,b]; b.neighbors = [a];\nconst copy = cloneGraph(a);\nconsole.log(copy !== a, copy.neighbors[0] !== b); //=> true true\nconsole.log(copy.neighbors[0] === copy.neighbors[1]); //=> true\nconsole.log(copy.neighbors[0].neighbors[0] === copy); //=> true\nconsole.log(cloneGraph(null)); //=> null' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Cloning visits the reachable component only. For that component, time is O(V+E), the clone itself occupies O(V+E), and the identity map plus queue use O(V) working space. Every original edge creates one corresponding edge between already established clone identities. Self-loops and parallel edges are preserved naturally. For Find Center of Star Graph, recognizing the promised structure can avoid general traversal: in a valid star with at least three vertices, the center is the common endpoint of the first two edges. That shortcut relies on the star guarantee. Do not apply it to an arbitrary graph simply because two edges happen to share an endpoint.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Two graph nodes both have value 7 but are distinct objects, and each points to the other. Why does a Map keyed by value corrupt a clone? Also, how many islands are in a 2×2 grid with land only at opposite corners under four-neighbor rules?', hints: ['A repeated label does not imply repeated identity.', 'A diagonal is not an edge under the chosen neighborhood.'], solution: 'A value-keyed map merges the two originals into one clone, turning their two-node cycle into an incorrect self-loop. Key by each original object reference so that two separate clones are created. The opposite-corner grid has two islands under four-neighbor rules; eight-neighbor rules would instead connect them.' }
    ] }
  ] },
  12: { sections: [
    { id: 'w12-directed-cycles', title: 'A directed cycle is a return to unfinished work', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Directed edges change the meaning of a cycle check. Imagine a task dependency A → B meaning A must finish before B can start. A route back from B to A makes the requirements impossible to satisfy. But seeing a previously visited vertex is not enough to prove such a cycle. In the diamond A → B → D and A → C → D, both branches reach D without any route back. We need to distinguish a vertex currently being explored from a vertex whose descendants are already finished. DFS supplies exactly this distinction through its active call path.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Use three colors: zero means undiscovered, one means active, and two means finished. On entry, color the vertex active. An edge to an active vertex is a back edge: it closes a directed path from that ancestor to the current vertex and back to the ancestor. An edge to a finished vertex is safe for this test, because that vertex is no longer an ancestor on the current path. After all outgoing edges have been checked, mark the current vertex finished. The outer loop must start DFS from every undiscovered vertex so that a cycle in a disconnected region is not missed.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function hasDirectedCycle(graph) {\n  const color = new Map();\n  const vertices = new Set(graph.keys());\n  for (const neighbors of graph.values()) for (const next of neighbors) vertices.add(next);\n  function visit(node) {\n    color.set(node, 1);\n    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      if (color.get(next) === 1) return true;\n      if (!color.has(next) && visit(next)) return true;\n    }\n    color.set(node, 2);\n    return false;\n  }\n  for (const node of vertices) if (!color.has(node) && visit(node)) return true;\n  return false;\n}\nconst diamond = new Map([["A",["B","C"]],["B",["D"]],["C",["D"]],["D",[]]]);\nconsole.log(hasDirectedCycle(diamond)); //=> false\nconst cycle = new Map([["A",["B"]],["B",["C"]],["C",["A"]]]);\nconsole.log(hasDirectedCycle(cycle)); //=> true\nconsole.log(hasDirectedCycle(new Map([["X",["X"]]]))); //=> true' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Trace A → B → C → A. Entering A, B, and C makes all three active. The final edge to A encounters color one, so the algorithm reports a cycle immediately. In the diamond, D becomes finished before the C branch encounters it, so that edge does not report a cycle. The proof also runs in reverse: any directed cycle reached by DFS must eventually encounter an already active vertex while following its edges. Each vertex is entered once and each outgoing edge is scanned once unless an early result ends the search. Worst-case time is O(V+E), colors require O(V), and recursion can require O(V) stack depth. For a deep graph in JavaScript, use explicit DFS frames or the iterative indegree method next.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Why does a single visited Set falsely report a cycle in A → B, A → C, B → D, C → D? What extra state distinguishes this graph from A → B → A?', hints: ['D can be complete before the C branch starts.', 'Only a destination still on the current DFS ancestry path proves a back edge.'], solution: 'The second edge reaching D finds it visited even though no route returns from D to an ancestor. Track active versus finished states. In the diamond, D is finished on the second encounter. In A → B → A, A remains active while B explores its outgoing edge, proving a directed cycle.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w12-topological-order', title: 'Topological sorting repeatedly removes available work', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'A topological order places every vertex before all destinations of its outgoing edges. It exists exactly when a directed graph is acyclic. This is not a numeric sort: labels can appear in any order consistent with dependencies, and several answers may be valid. Kahn’s algorithm starts with tasks having no unmet prerequisites, represented by indegree zero. Remove an available task, append it to the order, and decrement each dependent task’s indegree. When a dependent reaches zero, it becomes available. The indegree array is therefore changing state: it counts incoming edges from vertices that have not yet been removed.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Direction is the most common modeling error. In Course Schedule, an input pair [course, prerequisite] usually means prerequisite → course, not course → prerequisite. The reusable function below instead takes edges already expressed as [before, after]. Convert the problem’s input into that contract before calling it. Multiple identical edges are counted and decremented equally, so they do not create a false cycle. Isolated vertices begin ready immediately. A self-loop gives its vertex positive indegree that its own removal would be required to clear, making it an immediate example of impossible dependency ordering.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function topologicalOrder(n, edges) {\n  const graph = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);\n  const indegree = Array(n).fill(0);\n  for (const [before, after] of edges) {\n    graph[before].push(after);\n    indegree[after]++;\n  }\n  const ready = [];\n  for (let v = 0; v < n; v++) if (indegree[v] === 0) ready.push(v);\n  const order = [];\n  for (let head = 0; head < ready.length; head++) {\n    const node = ready[head];\n    order.push(node);\n    for (const next of graph[node]) {\n      indegree[next]--;\n      if (indegree[next] === 0) ready.push(next);\n    }\n  }\n  return order.length === n ? order : null;\n}\nfunction canFinishCourses(n, prerequisites) {\n  return topologicalOrder(n, prerequisites.map(([course, prerequisite]) => [prerequisite, course])) !== null;\n}\nconsole.log(topologicalOrder(4, [[0,1],[0,2],[1,3],[2,3]])); //=> [0,1,2,3]\nconsole.log(topologicalOrder(3, [[0,1],[1,0]])); //=> null\nconsole.log(topologicalOrder(0, [])); //=> []\nconsole.log(canFinishCourses(2, [[1,0]])); //=> true' },
      { type: 'diagram', text: 'Edges: 0→1, 0→2, 1→3, 2→3\nInitial indegrees [0,1,1,2]; ready [0]\nRemove 0:         [0,0,0,2]; append 1,2\nRemove 1:         [0,0,0,1]; 3 still waits\nRemove 2:         [0,0,0,0]; append 3\nRemove 3: all four vertices output\nA cycle 1→2→1 never releases either vertex.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Correctness follows from the remaining-graph invariant. A zero-indegree vertex has no remaining predecessor, so appending it cannot place it before an unmet prerequisite. Removing its outgoing edges maintains the same invariant for the next iteration. Why does failure to output all vertices prove a cycle? If every remaining vertex has at least one incoming edge from another remaining vertex, repeatedly follow incoming edges. A finite vertex set forces some vertex to repeat, producing a directed cycle. Conversely, vertices in a directed cycle can never be the first one removed from that cycle, since each still has an incoming edge from its predecessor.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Building lists and indegrees takes O(V+E). Every vertex enters ready at most once, and every edge causes one decrement, so total time remains O(V+E). Graph storage is O(V+E), while indegrees, queue, and output each use O(V). The queue shown retains processed entries; that still matches the O(V) bound. If the task asks for the lexicographically smallest valid order, a FIFO queue does not provide that guarantee: choose the smallest currently available label with a min-heap, adding logarithmic queue costs. Cycle detection alone needs only whether all vertices were removed. To return an actual cycle witness, the earlier active-path DFS can retain parent links.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'For vertices 0,1,2,3 with edges 0→1, 1→2, 2→1 and no edges touching 3, compute initial indegrees, possible queue removals, and the cycle verdict. Does every unremoved vertex necessarily lie on a cycle?', hints: ['Both 0 and 3 are initially available.', 'Add an edge 2→4 in a larger graph to test the final claim.'], solution: 'Initial indegrees are [0,2,1,0]. Removing 0 lowers indegree(1) to one; 3 can also be removed. Vertices 1 and 2 remain, so fewer than four outputs proves a cycle. In general an unremoved vertex may only depend on a cycle: an added vertex 4 reached from 2 also remains despite not belonging to any directed cycle itself.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w12-dijkstra', title: 'Dijkstra turns tentative routes into settled shortest distances', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Weighted shortest paths require more information than BFS layers. Let distance[v] mean the cheapest complete route discovered so far from the source to v. Initially the source has distance zero and every other vertex has Infinity. Relaxing edge u → v with weight w asks whether distance[u] + w improves distance[v]. If so, replace the distance and record u as predecessor. The crucial choice is which vertex to process next. Dijkstra always removes the smallest tentative distance from a min-priority queue, and its proof requires every edge weight to be nonnegative. Zero-weight edges are allowed; negative edges are not.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Consider A → B with cost 10, A → C with cost 1, C → B with cost 2, and B → D with cost 1. Expanding A discovers tentative B=10 and C=1. C is next, improving B to 3. B at distance 3 improves D to 4. A queue that marks B permanently visited when first enqueued would freeze the wrong answer 10. Discovery is not settlement. A simple heap implementation can leave the old entry (10,B) in the queue and insert (3,B) separately. When the old entry eventually appears, compare its distance to the current best and skip it. This is the lazy stale-entry technique.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'class DistanceHeap {\n  constructor() { this.a = []; }\n  get size() { return this.a.length; }\n  push(entry) {\n    const a = this.a;\n    a.push(entry);\n    let i = a.length - 1;\n    while (i > 0) {\n      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);\n      if (a[p][0] <= a[i][0]) break;\n      [a[p],a[i]] = [a[i],a[p]];\n      i = p;\n    }\n  }\n  pop() {\n    const a = this.a, result = a[0], last = a.pop();\n    if (a.length === 0) return result;\n    a[0] = last;\n    let i = 0;\n    while (2 * i + 1 < a.length) {\n      let c = 2 * i + 1;\n      if (c + 1 < a.length && a[c + 1][0] < a[c][0]) c++;\n      if (a[i][0] <= a[c][0]) break;\n      [a[i],a[c]] = [a[c],a[i]];\n      i = c;\n    }\n    return result;\n  }\n}\nfunction dijkstra(graph, source) {\n  const vertices = new Set(graph.keys());\n  for (const edges of graph.values()) {\n    for (const [next,weight] of edges) {\n      if (!Number.isFinite(weight) || weight < 0) throw new RangeError("Weights must be finite and nonnegative");\n      vertices.add(next);\n    }\n  }\n  if (!vertices.has(source)) throw new Error("Unknown source");\n  const distance = new Map([...vertices].map(v => [v, Infinity]));\n  const parent = new Map();\n  const pending = new DistanceHeap();\n  distance.set(source, 0);\n  pending.push([0,source]);\n  while (pending.size > 0) {\n    const [cost,node] = pending.pop();\n    if (cost !== distance.get(node)) continue;\n    for (const [next,weight] of graph.get(node) ?? []) {\n      const candidate = cost + weight;\n      if (!Number.isFinite(candidate)) throw new RangeError("Distance overflow");\n      if (candidate < distance.get(next)) {\n        distance.set(next, candidate);\n        parent.set(next, node);\n        pending.push([candidate,next]);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return { distance, parent };\n}\nfunction routeTo(parent, source, target) {\n  const path = [target];\n  let node = target;\n  while (node !== source) {\n    if (!parent.has(node)) return null;\n    node = parent.get(node);\n    path.push(node);\n  }\n  return path.reverse();\n}\nconst graph = new Map([\n  ["A",[["B",10],["C",1]]], ["B",[["D",1]]],\n  ["C",[["B",2],["D",8]]], ["D",[]], ["E",[]]\n]);\nconst result = dijkstra(graph, "A");\nconsole.log([...result.distance.values()]); //=> [0,3,1,4,Infinity]\nconsole.log(routeTo(result.parent, "A", "D")); //=> ["A","C","B","D"]\nconsole.log(routeTo(result.parent, "A", "E")); //=> null\nconsole.log(routeTo(result.parent, "A", "A")); //=> ["A"]' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The greedy proof is about the first unsettled vertex on a hypothetical better route. Suppose u is removed with the smallest current distance, but some shorter source-to-u route exists. Follow that route from the settled region to its first unsettled vertex x. Its predecessor was already processed, so relaxation gave x a tentative distance no larger than the cost of that route prefix. Nonnegative remaining edges mean that prefix cannot cost more than the full shorter route to u. Thus x would have a tentative distance smaller than u, contradicting u being chosen next. This is the exact point where negative weights break the argument: a later edge could make a costly prefix become a cheap full route.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'For example, A → B costs 2, A → C costs 5, and C → B costs −10. Settling B at 2 would be wrong because the route through C costs −5. Use an algorithm with a suitable negative-edge guarantee, such as Bellman–Ford, for that problem family. Do not infer that undirected graphs make negative weights safe: traversing a negative undirected edge in both directions creates a negative-cost walk cycle. Our code rejects negative and nonfinite weights before searching, even in unreachable regions, making its contract explicit. Numeric sums must remain representable; exact large-integer costs may require a separately designed BigInt implementation rather than ordinary Number arithmetic.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Be precise about heap complexity. With an indexed heap supporting decrease-key, there are O(V) heap entries and the standard bound is O((V+E) log V) time with O(V) queue space. This self-contained lazy implementation can insert O(E+1) entries, so its more general bound is O(V+E+(E+1) log(E+2)) time and O(V+E) auxiliary storage including tentative maps and pending entries. For simple graphs, E is at most V², so log E is O(log V), recovering the familiar O((V+E) log V) form. Parallel edges make the explicit E-based bound more honest. Repeatedly sorting and shifting a plain array is not a logarithmic priority queue.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The strict improvement test prevents equal-cost alternatives, including zero-weight cycles, from endlessly inserting duplicate work. Predecessors describe one shortest route, not every possible shortest route. If only one target is needed, stopping when a nonstale target entry is popped is safe; stopping when the target is first discovered is not. Network Delay Time runs the source search to completion and takes the maximum distance over all network vertices, returning failure if any remains Infinity. Include isolated vertices in the graph so this check cannot accidentally ignore them. Directed edges remain directed unless the input explicitly allows travel in both directions.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Trace Dijkstra from S on S→A(8), S→B(2), B→A(1), A→T(2), B→T(9). List the final distances and stale entries. Explain why marking visited when inserting A gives the wrong answer.', hints: ['After B is removed, A improves from 8 to 3.', 'After A at 3 is removed, T improves from 11 to 5.'], solution: 'Distances are S=0, B=2, A=3, T=5. The queue may retain obsolete entries (8,A) and (11,T), which are skipped because their costs no longer match the maps. Marking A final on its first insertion would preserve 8 and block the cheaper route S,B,A. Finality follows minimum-distance removal under nonnegative weights, not initial discovery.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'w12-recognition-and-itinerary', title: 'Choose the graph question before choosing the algorithm', blocks: [
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Graph prompts often share the same nouns while asking for different mathematics. “Can I reach this vertex?” needs traversal. “Fewest equal-cost steps?” suggests BFS. “What prerequisite order works?” suggests topological sorting. “Cheapest nonnegative route?” suggests Dijkstra. “Use every ticket exactly once?” asks about edges rather than shortest routes or prerequisite ordering. Read what must be consumed, minimized, or ordered before selecting a template. A visited-vertex Set is excellent for reachability, but disastrous when a valid answer may revisit a vertex while consuming a different edge. Reconstruct Itinerary, listed in the existing syllabus, is that last kind of problem.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'An itinerary uses each directed ticket once, so repeated airports are allowed and duplicate tickets are distinct edges. Under the usual guarantee that a valid itinerary from JFK exists, Hierholzer’s algorithm builds an Euler trail. Follow available edges until stuck, then add the dead-end airport to a reverse route while unwinding. Sorting destinations in reverse lexical order lets pop choose the smallest available destination. The final reversal splices detours into a route that consumes all tickets. This is not a topological order: valid itineraries may contain cycles, and the number of output airports is one greater than the number of tickets.' },
      { type: 'code', code: 'function reconstructItinerary(tickets, start = "JFK") {\n  // Contract: a complete valid itinerary from start exists.\n  const graph = new Map();\n  for (const [from,to] of tickets) {\n    if (!graph.has(from)) graph.set(from, []);\n    graph.get(from).push(to);\n  }\n  for (const destinations of graph.values()) destinations.sort().reverse();\n  const stack = [start], reverseRoute = [];\n  while (stack.length > 0) {\n    const airport = stack[stack.length - 1];\n    const destinations = graph.get(airport);\n    if (destinations?.length > 0) stack.push(destinations.pop());\n    else reverseRoute.push(stack.pop());\n  }\n  return reverseRoute.reverse();\n}\nconsole.log(reconstructItinerary([["JFK","KUL"],["JFK","NRT"],["NRT","JFK"]])); //=> ["JFK","NRT","JFK","KUL"]\nconsole.log(reconstructItinerary([["JFK","A"],["JFK","A"],["A","JFK"]])); //=> ["JFK","A","JFK","A"]' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'Trace the first example carefully. Choosing KUL first reaches a dead end, so KUL enters the reverse route. Back at JFK, the remaining edge to NRT leads back to JFK, whose edges are now exhausted. Unwinding gives reverseRoute [KUL,JFK,NRT,JFK]; reversing produces JFK,NRT,JFK,KUL. The tempting direct greedy output JFK,KUL would strand tickets, but deferred output places that dead-end segment at the end. Each ticket is removed once and each stack occurrence is removed once, giving O(E) traversal after sorting. Sorting all outgoing lists takes O(E log(E+1)) worst-case time; adjacency, stack, and output use O(E+1) space.' },
      { type: 'paragraph', text: 'The validity guarantee is substantive. Without it, Hierholzer-style output can fail to be a legal complete itinerary; checking only route length is insufficient on arbitrary directed input. A general API should verify Euler-trail degree and connectivity conditions or validate the returned consecutive edges against the ticket multiset. That validation is outside this example’s stated contract. The edge-splicing invariant explains completeness under the guarantee: every finished segment exhausts its chosen edges and is joined at a shared airport. Choosing the smallest outgoing destination during traversal, while deferring segments that would strand remaining edges, gives the lexical itinerary for the promised problem. This advanced example is a bridge from the syllabus practice title, not a new assignment or a substitute for the shortest-path proof.' },
      { type: 'exercise', prompt: 'Choose an algorithm and justify its prerequisite for each request: minimum number of moves in a maze; schedule all prerequisites; cheapest route with tolls of zero or more; use every flight ticket once. Explain why a topological sort cannot solve the final request.', hints: ['Ask whether vertices or edges must be visited and whether weights matter.', 'A round trip can be valid travel even though it is a directed cycle.'], solution: 'Use BFS for equal-cost maze moves, Kahn’s algorithm or DFS finishing order for an acyclic prerequisite graph, Dijkstra for nonnegative tolls, and an Euler-trail algorithm for consuming every ticket. Topological sorting orders vertices and requires acyclicity; an itinerary consumes edges, can revisit airports, and can legitimately include cycles.' }
    ] }
  ] }
};
